Result in one page

The Pantheon pass produced two connected but independently useful prime-pattern families.

1. Gaussian Crowns. Every pair of distinct odd primes has canonical center–radius coordinates that lift it into a primitive Pythagorean triple. The hypotenuse
  [
K(p,q)=\frac{p^2+q^2}{2}
]
  is the pair’s Gaussian crown. Reapplying (K) gives the nonlinear crown recurrence
  [
x_{n+2}=\frac{x_n^2+x_{n+1}^2}{2}.
]
  The recurrence has exact invariants: every new term is coprime to the previous two; every prime factor of a new term is (1\pmod 4); and a non-exceptional orbit that passes two prime crowns falls permanently into the residue (1\pmod{60}). A scan of all 5,761,453 consecutive-prime seeds below (10^8) found five seeds with four successive prime/probable-prime crowns and no seed with five.
2. Mirror Carriers. A factor shared by the two integers mirrored around a center is not mysterious: after removing mandatory parity, it is exactly a factor shared by the center and the offset. This converts factorization into visible stripes. Inside a consecutive-prime gap, those stripes measure how much of the prime-free interval is certified by factors of its midpoint. The resulting carrier load has an analytic residue-preserving null model. Across 63 gap classes with at least 100 samples below (10^8), predicted and observed mean loads have Pearson correlation (0.999463) and mean absolute error (0.004746).

The crown recurrence, its (60)-attractor theorem, the carrier-load metric, its null law, and the seven rigid load genotypes were not found in the targeted web/arXiv/OEIS audit. That is evidence of independent novelty, not proof that no one has ever written an equivalent construction.

