2. Prime-pair hinge coordinates

Let (p<q) be distinct odd primes and define

[
c=\frac{p+q}{2},\qquad h=\frac{q-p}{2}.
]

Then (p=c-h) and (q=c+h).

Hinge Orthogonality Theorem

For every distinct odd-prime pair:

[
\gcd(c,h)=1,
]

and (c,h) have opposite parity.

Proof. Any common divisor of (c) and (h) divides both (c-h=p) and (c+h=q). Distinct primes are coprime, so the divisor is (1). Since (c+h=q) is odd, (c) and (h) have opposite parity. (square)

This is elementary, but the coordinate change exposes several useful patterns at once.

The rotated wheel

For any prime modulus (ell) not equal to either endpoint,

[
c\not\equiv h\pmod\ell,
\qquad
c\not\equiv-h\pmod\ell.
]

The two forbidden diagonals contain (2\ell-1) points in the (ell\times\ell) residue grid, leaving

[
\ell^2-(2\ell-1)=(\ell-1)^2
]

endpoint-safe center–radius states. This is the ordinary two-endpoint wheel sieve rotated by (45^circ), but the rotation turns endpoint exclusions into visible cross-shaped geometry.

Two small moduli become especially crisp:

• (ell=2): only opposite-parity ((c,h)) states survive.
• (ell=3): for (p,q>3), exactly one of (c,h) is divisible by (3). There are not enough nonzero residues for both coordinates to avoid the two diagonals.

That ternary switch later explains the strong (g\bmod 6) oscillation in midpoint carrier load.

