3. The prime-pair Pythagorean lift

Hinge orthogonality makes ((c,h)) valid Euclidean parameters for a primitive Pythagorean triple:

[
T(p,q)=
\left(
pq,
\frac{q^2-p^2}{2},
\frac{p^2+q^2}{2}
\right).
]

Indeed,

[
c^2-h^2=pq,
\qquad
2ch=\frac{q^2-p^2}{2},
\qquad
c^2+h^2=\frac{p^2+q^2}{2}.
]

Because (c,h) are coprime and opposite in parity, the triple is primitive.

This is the nontrivial factor-split lift of the semiprime odd leg (pq): its Euclidean factors (c-h) and (c+h) are the two source primes. It is not the only primitive triple with odd leg (pq); the factor split (1\cdot pq) gives another. The prime pair is recovered exactly from this lift by (p=c-h, q=c+h).

Several triangle invariants retain the prime-pair grammar:

[
\begin{aligned}
\text{odd leg} &= pq,\
\text{even leg} &= (p+q)(q-p)/2,\
\text{hypotenuse} &= (p^2+q^2)/2,\
\text{perimeter} &= q(p+q),\
\text{inradius} &= p(q-p)/2=ph,\
\text{area} &= pqch.
\end{aligned}
]

The Euclidean parametrization itself is classical; see, for example, work on constructive representations of primitive Pythagorean triples. The prime-pair compiler and the recurrence below are the new objects being proposed here.

