4. Gaussian crowns

Define the crown of a coprime odd pair by

[
K(a,b)=\frac{a^2+b^2}{2}.
]

If (c=(a+b)/2) and (h=(b-a)/2), then

[
K(a,b)=c^2+h^2=N(c+ih),
]

the Gaussian norm of the hinge coordinate (c+ih). For a prime pair, it is also the hypotenuse of the canonical lift.

Crown Factor-Gate Theorem

If (a,b) are coprime odd integers, then:

1. (K(a,b)) is an odd integer and (K(a,b)\equiv1\pmod4).
2. (gcd(K(a,b),a)=gcd(K(a,b),b)=1).
3. Every rational prime factor of (K(a,b)) is (1\pmod4).

Proof. Odd squares are (1\pmod8), so their half-sum is (1\pmod4). Because (2) is invertible modulo odd (a) and (b), a common factor of (K) and (a) would also divide (b^2), contradicting (gcd(a,b)=1); similarly for (b). Finally, if a prime (r\equiv3\pmod4) divided (a^2+b^2), coprimality would make (a/b) a square root of (-1\pmod r), which does not exist for (r\equiv3\pmod4). (square)

Composite crowns are therefore unusually constrained. Examples from terminated chains confirm the gate:

[
12469=37\cdot337,
]

and

[
252025=5^2\cdot17\cdot593;
]

every displayed factor is (1\pmod4).

