5. The crown recurrence

Put the crown operator on the Wrestling Mat:

[
x_{n+2}=K(x_n,x_{n+1})
=\frac{x_n^2+x_{n+1}^2}{2}.
]

Starting from coprime odd integers, every term remains odd and every adjacent pair remains coprime. More strongly,

[
\gcd(x_{n+2},x_n)=gcd(x_{n+2},x_{n+1})=1.
]

The recurrence therefore has two-step local orthogonality. A failure may reuse a factor from a more distant ancestor, but never from either parent.

The (60)-Attractor Theorem

Let (x_0,x_1>5) be primes. If the first two crowns (x_2,x_3) are also prime, then

[
x_n\equiv1\pmod{60}
\qquad\text{for every }n\ge2,
]

whether or not later terms remain prime.

Proof.

• Modulo (3), every nonzero square is (1), so (K(a,b)\equiv1\pmod3).
• Every crown is (1\pmod4) by the factor-gate theorem.
• Modulo (5), nonzero squares are (1) or (4). For (x_2) not to be divisible by (5), the two seed squares must match. If both were (4), then (x_2\equiv4), hence (x_2^2\equiv1), and the next crown would combine square classes (4) and (1), making (x_3\equiv0\pmod5). Since (x_3>5) is prime, that is impossible. Therefore the seed squares are both (1), (x_2\equiv1\pmod5), (x_3\equiv1\pmod5), and the state (1,1) is fixed thereafter.

The Chinese remainder theorem combines (1\pmod3), (1\pmod4), and (1\pmod5) into (1\pmod{60}). (square)

This is a real attractor, not a visual analogy: survival through a primality gate collapses all future modular states into a single residue class.

