6. Mirror bonds

For an odd center (n) and (0<k<n), define the raw mirror bond

[
B_n(k)=\gcd(n-k,n+k).
]

Mirror Factorization Identity

[
B_n(k)=
\begin{cases}
\gcd(n,k),&k\text{ even},\
2\gcd(n,k),&k\text{ odd}.
\end{cases}
]

Thus, after removing mandatory parity, the shared factors of the mirrored pair are exactly the shared factors of center and offset.

Proof sketch. Write (d=\gcd(n,k)), factor (d) from (n-k) and (n+k), and note that the reduced sum and difference are coprime except for a factor (2) precisely when the reduced offset is odd. Since (n) and (d) are odd, this is equivalent to (k) being odd. (square)

Bondless-Mirror Primality Criterion

An odd integer (n>1) is prime if and only if its parity-normalized mirror bonds are all (1) for

[
1\le k\le\sqrt n.
]

This is trial division rotated into mirror space, not a faster primality test. Its value is architectural: factors become reusable mirror stripes rather than isolated divisibility facts.

