7. Prime-gap carrier load

Let (p<q) be consecutive odd primes, with center (c), radius (h), and gap (g=2h). There are (h-1) odd composite integers strictly between the endpoints.

For each (1\le x<h) such that (c\pm x) are odd, the mirrored sites share an odd factor precisely when

[
\gcd(c,x)>1.
]

Define the midpoint carrier load

[
\Lambda(p,q)=
\frac{
\mathbf 1_{c\text{ odd}}
+2#{1\le x<h:\ x\equiv h\pmod2,\ \gcd(c,x)>1}
}{h-1}.
]

It measures the fraction of interior odd composites certified by factors of the midpoint itself. The remainder requires split flank certificates: different factors on the left and right.

Carrier Selection Rule

Fix the radius (h) and an odd prime (r<h).

• If (r\mid h), endpoint primality forces (r\nmid c).
• If (r\nmid h), then among the (r-2) midpoint residues for which neither endpoint is divisible by (r), exactly one is (c\equiv0\pmod r).
• Therefore
  [
\Pr(r\mid c\mid c\pm h\not\equiv0\pmod r)=\frac1{r-2}.
]
• At (r=3), that probability is (1): when (3\nmid h), factor (3) is a forced midpoint carrier.

This is the carrier form of the rotated wheel.

Residue-preserving null law

Distinct primes are independent under the Chinese remainder theorem, giving the endpoint-conditioned expectation

[
\mathbb E_0[\Lambda_h]
=\frac{
\mathbf1_{2\mid h}
+2\displaystyle\sum_{\substack{1\le x<h\x\equiv h\ (2)}}
\left[
1-\prod_{\substack{r\mid x,\ r\text{ odd}\r\nmid h}}
\left(1-\frac1{r-2}\right)
\right]
}{h-1}.
]

This null model retains endpoint-safe residue structure but does not assume the interval is actually a consecutive-prime gap.

