9. Conjectures and next attacks

These are intentionally separated from the theorems and finite computations.

C1. Crown Finiteness Conjecture

Every coprime odd seed (a,b>1) eventually produces a composite under

[
x_{n+2}=(x_n^2+x_{n+1}^2)/2.
]

The rapid digit doubling makes an infinite all-prime orbit probabilistically implausible, but that is not a proof.

C2. Consecutive-Prime Crown Record Problem

Let (R(X)) be the longest prime-crown run from consecutive-prime seeds (p<q\le X). The scan gives

[
R(10^8)=4
]

at the probable-prime standard used here. Find the first seed with five certified crowns, or prove a modular obstruction.

C3. Modular Survivor Graphs

For every prime (r\equiv1\pmod4), build the finite state graph of the crown recurrence modulo (r), delete states that hit zero, and classify its survivor attractors. Modulo (5), survival through two crowns collapses to the fixed state ((1,1)). The same analysis at (13,17,29,\ldots) may produce stronger necessary congruence corridors for long crown runs.

C4. Gap-Closure Premium

For each gap size (g), define

[ \Delta_g(X)= \mathbb E[\Lambda\mid p,q\le X\text{ consecutive},q-p=g]

\mathbb E[\Lambda\mid p,q\le X\text{ prime},q-p=g].
]

The sign is not universally positive. At (X=2\cdot10^7), examples include approximately (+0.02024) for (g=14), (+0.01915) for (g=22), and (-0.01078) for (g=66). Determine whether each fixed-gap premium converges, and whether its sign follows from the interior admissible-tuple geometry.

C5. Carrier Extremizers

At a fixed scale and minimum gap, characterize the centers maximizing (Lambda). Primorial and near-primorial centers are natural candidates because they muster many reusable factors, but the two endpoint constraints and the (pm1) leaks prevent a trivial answer.

